You are building a small, honest C++17 options-pricing library for a Warwick Data Science student’s GitHub. Name the repo folder option-pricer. This is a learning/recruiting artefact, not a trading system.

Goal. From-scratch pricer for vanilla equity options. Three methods for the same contract, checked against identities and against each other — not a Kaggle score and not a QuantLib wrapper.

Methods

Black–Scholes–Merton closed form for European call and put (price + Greeks: delta, gamma, vega, theta, rho).
Cox–Ross–Rubinstein binomial tree: European first, then American (early exercise).
Monte Carlo for European only: GBM, risk-neutral, sample mean of discounted payoff, report price and standard error. Seedable RNG.
Contract. One spec: S, K, r, q, sigma, T, option type call/put, exercise European/American. q is continuous dividend yield (allow 0). No barriers, no Asians, no local vol, no rates curves.

Hard constraints

C++17, CMake 3.16+, no QuantLib, no Boost.Math unless you really need erf and then say so. Prefer the standard library.
No raw new/delete. RAII, std::vector, <random>, headers that compile.
No live market data, no scraping, no GPU, no SIMD, no GUI, no neural nets, no “matching engine”, no Excel COM.
Do not claim calibration to listed options. Do not mention HFT or production trading.
UK English in comments and README.
Keep it small: a recruiter should understand it in 10 minutes.
